    'ECL Processing' => [

        'Running an ECL calculation' => [
            'body' => '<p>The expected credit loss (ECL) calculation is the step that turns the loan book, the models and the forward-looking adjustment into a provision figure. It reads every loan in the reporting period you choose, multiplies exposure by probability of default and loss given default, optionally discounts the result at the loan\'s effective interest rate, and writes the answer to each loan and to a summary row per stage. Open the sidebar, expand <b>ECL Processing</b>, choose <b>ECL Calculation</b>, then press <b>Calculate ECL</b>. The page that opens is titled <b>ECL - Input Essentials</b>.</p><p>Run this only after the loan book for the period is imported and reconciled, staging is settled, and the PD and LGD models for the period are calculated and applied. If any of those are missing the run still completes, but it measures the wrong thing.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Loan Book Period</b>. A month picker. Choose the month-end snapshot you are measuring, for example November 2025.</li><li><b>ECL Calculation Level</b>. Either <b>Portfolio Level</b> or <b>Sector Level</b>. This decides how the results are grouped and stored.</li><li><b>Portfolio Group</b>. Shown only when the level is Portfolio. A list of the portfolios you set up under Portfolio Setup. Leave it on <b>Select Portfolio</b> to run every portfolio, or pick one.</li><li><b>Sector</b>. Shown only when the level is Sector. Each entry shows the sector code and name, for example <code>1 - Agriculture, forestry and fishing</code>.</li><li><b>PD Type</b>. <b>PD Before FLI</b> uses the probability of default straight from the transition matrix. <b>PD After FLI</b> uses the probability after the forward-looking adjustment. Use PD After FLI for a reported figure; use PD Before FLI to see what the adjustment contributed.</li><li><b>LGD Type</b>. <b>Customer LGD</b>, <b>Collection LGD</b>, or <b>Both (customer x collection)</b>, which multiplies the two.</li><li><b>ECL Measurement Basis</b>. Two cards you choose between. <b>Discounted ECL, EIR present value</b> discounts the expected loss using the approved, locked effective interest rate and keeps the undiscounted amount as a control. <b>Undiscounted ECL, no present-value adjustment</b> calculates exposure times probability times loss rate with no interest rate applied, which is useful for comparison and transition analysis. When you pick the discounted basis the page warns you in amber that loans without a locked EIR or a usable discount horizon will remain visible as unresolved exceptions.</li><li><b>Calculate ECL</b> starts the run. <b>Back</b> returns to the results list without running anything.</li></ul><h4>What happens next</h4><ul><li>Each loan in scope receives its own ECL value, and, on the discounted basis, a discounted value, a discounting effect, the rate used, the rate source and a discount status.</li><li>A summary row is written for each IFRS 9 stage with the total exposure, the total ECL and the average probability and loss rate used, tagged with the level and the identifier you chose.</li><li>The reporting period is marked as having a calculated ECL, which is what makes it appear in the dashboard and the IFRS 9 Reports hub period lists.</li><li>The run is recorded in the audit trail with your name and the options you chose.</li></ul><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>An ECL calculation is already running.</b> The system takes a lock for each period and scope so two people cannot write the same figures at once. Wait for the other run to finish and try again. If a run was interrupted and the message will not clear, ask Dupleix to release the lock.</li><li><b>Records could not be discounted.</b> Expected on the discounted basis when a contract has no approved and locked EIR. The count appears on the results page. Resolve the contracts on the EIR Coverage and Blockers screen, then run again.</li><li><b>Every figure is zero.</b> The PD or LGD model for the period has not been applied to the loan book. Apply the transition matrix and the LGD run first.</li></ul>',
