        'ECL projections (time-phased)' => [
            'body' => '<p>The <b>Time-phased ECL Projection Audit</b> shows the month-by-month working behind a discounted ECL figure, so a reviewer or an auditor can see how the number was built rather than taking it on trust. Open <b>ECL Processing</b>, choose <b>ECL Calculation</b>, then press <b>Projection Audit</b>.</p><p>The engine projects a monthly curve for each contract across the approved economic scenarios, works out the cash shortfall expected in each month, discounts it at the contract\'s locked effective interest rate, and weights the scenarios by their approved probabilities. This screen shows both the answer and every step.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Contract totals</b>. One row per contract, with columns <b>Contract</b>, <b>Stage</b>, <b>Undiscounted</b>, <b>Discounted</b> and <b>Horizon</b>. The horizon is how many months the projection ran for.</li><li><b>Projection evidence</b>. The detail table, with columns <b>Contract</b>, <b>Scenario</b>, <b>Period</b>, <b>Date</b>, <b>Opening EAD</b>, <b>Marginal PD</b>, <b>LGD</b>, <b>Shortfall</b>, <b>Factor</b> and <b>Weighted discounted</b>. One row per contract, scenario and month.</li></ul><h4>How to read a row</h4><ul><li><b>Opening EAD</b> is the exposure at the start of that month. For a performing loan it falls as the contractual schedule repays; for a defaulted (stage 3) loan it does not amortise, because a borrower in default is not making those instalments.</li><li><b>Marginal PD</b> is the chance of default in that month alone, not the cumulative chance. Summed with survival across the horizon it reproduces the lifetime probability.</li><li><b>LGD</b> is the loss rate applied to that month.</li><li><b>Shortfall</b> is the cash the platform expects not to receive in that month.</li><li><b>Factor</b> is the discount factor for that month, derived from the contract\'s effective interest rate.</li><li><b>Weighted discounted</b> is the shortfall multiplied by the discount factor and by the scenario weight. The sum of this column for a contract is its discounted ECL.</li></ul><h4>Behaviour worth knowing</h4><ul><li><b>Stage 1</b> projects twelve months. <b>Stage 2</b> projects the remaining life, and the monthly hazard is anchored to the twelve-month probability the loan tape carries, so a longer life compounds to a higher lifetime probability rather than staying flat.</li><li><b>Stage 3</b> exposure does not amortise. Where a reviewed recovery plan exists for the contract, the shortfall is placed on the dates the plan names instead of at the end of the schedule.</li><li>Contracts the engine cannot resolve, because no locked EIR exists, keep the undiscounted figure and are reported as unresolved rather than being given a default rate.</li></ul><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>A contract is missing from the list.</b> It was not discounted, so there is no projection to show. Check its discount status on the ECL results screen and its blockers on the EIR Coverage screen.</li><li><b>Only one scenario appears.</b> Only the approved scenario assumptions are used. If the base, upside and downside weights have not been approved, the projection runs on what exists.</li></ul>',
